INFORMATION-ADAPTIVE COMPUTATION


A First-Principles Framework for Value-Driven Allocation of Computational Resources

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatgpt, Qwen, Grok XAi, 

## Abstract

Much of the dominant inference computation in contemporary foundation models remains predetermined by the architecture and decoding procedure, with adaptive allocation occurring only in limited or modular forms. While recent works have explored adaptive computation in isolation—byte-level dynamic patching, adaptive computation time, conditional routing through Mixture-of-Experts and Mixture-of-Depths, test-time deliberation in the style of o-series reasoning models, and rational metareasoning—these approaches adapt individual dimensions of computation independently. In this thesis, we introduce **Information-Adaptive Computation (IAC)**, a unified theoretical framework that treats computation as a scarce resource to be allocated according to estimated marginal value. We formalize the problem around a central object: the **Compute-Value Function** $V_C(s)$, which simultaneously (i) makes the Value-of-Computation objective computable without requiring knowledge of the true outcome, (ii) enables temporal credit assignment across allocation decisions, and (iii) provides a principled stopping rule derived from the same principle as allocation. We distinguish four types of information complexity—statistical, epistemic, causal, and decisional—and argue that optimal allocation must respond to marginal computational value rather than any single proxy. We identify four fundamental mathematical challenges: the differentiability of coupled discrete allocation decisions, the cost-reliability trade-off of uncertainty estimation, temporal credit assignment (reframed as central rather than peripheral), and inference-time distribution shift of the epistemic estimator. We propose a rigorous experimental paradigm to measure the compute-utility Pareto frontier and to test whether learned allocation policies develop a quantifiable per-input correlation between compute expenditure and input asymptotic complexity. We explicitly analyze failure modes—compute oscillation, budget blow-up, and allocator-induced overhead—and frame preliminary regret guarantees as a conjecture requiring state abstraction. We sketch what an IAC scaling law might look like: a frontier shift in utility-per-FLOP as a function of allocator quality. Our central hypothesis is that shifting from fixed-representation processing to value-driven investigation will redefine the efficiency frontier of artificial intelligence.

