## APPENDIX C: Hardware-Aware Cost Model Specification

We formalize the proposed cost function $C(a) = \alpha C_{\text{FLOP}}(a) + \beta C_{\text{HBM}}(a)$ for a standard Transformer architecture. This specification is intended as a design template for future implementation; the specific constants $\alpha, \beta$ will depend on the target hardware and must be calibrated empirically via micro-benchmarks.

### C.1 Notation

| Symbol | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| $h$ | Hidden dimension |
| $L$ | Total number of transformer layers |
| $S$ | Sequence length (or number of patches) |
| $E$ | Number of experts (MoE) |
| $k$ | Number of activated experts (top-$k$) |
| $W_{\text{layer}}$ | Parameter footprint per layer (bytes) |
| $B_{\text{HBM}}$ | Peak HBM bandwidth (bytes/sec) |
| $F_{\text{peak}}$ | Peak FLOPS (ops/sec) |

### C.2 FLOP Cost ($C_{\text{FLOP}}$)

The arithmetic cost of a single standard Transformer layer (self-attention + FFN) for a sequence of length $S$ is approximately:
$$ C_{\text{FLOP}}^{\text{layer}}(S) \approx 12 h^2 S + 8 h S^2 $$
where the first term corresponds to the FFN and projection matrices, and the second to the attention computation.

| Action | FLOP Cost |
|--------|-----------|
| $\Delta d = +1$ (add one layer) | $12 h^2 S + 8 h S^2$ |
| $\Delta r$ (halve patch size, $S \to 2S$) | Change in attention: $8h(2S)^2 - 8hS^2 = 24hS^2$ |
| $\Delta p$ (skip one layer) | $-(12 h^2 S + 8 h S^2)$ (savings) |
| $\Delta e$ (activate $k$ of $E$ experts) | $k \cdot 12 h^2 S$ (FFN only; attention shared) |
| $\Delta i$ (retrieve $S_{\text{ext}}$ tokens) | $8 h S_{\text{ext}}^2 + 8 h S \cdot S_{\text{ext}}$ (cross-attention) |
| $\text{STOP}$ | $0$ |

### C.3 Memory Bandwidth Cost ($C_{\text{HBM}}$)

In memory-bound regimes (which dominate inference for large models on modern accelerators), the cost is governed by loading weights from HBM to SRAM:
$$ C_{\text{HBM}}(a) = \frac{\text{Bytes loaded from HBM for action } a}{B_{\text{HBM}}} $$

| Action | HBM Cost | Notes |
|--------|----------|-------|
| $\Delta d = +1$ | $W_{\text{layer}} / B_{\text{HBM}}$ | Must load full layer weights |
| $\Delta p$ (skip layer) | $0$ if early routing; $W_{\text{layer}} / B_{\text{HBM}}$ if late routing | **Critical distinction:** early routing (decision based on previous layer's output) avoids loading skipped layers; late routing loads then discards |
| $\Delta e$ (MoE) | $k \cdot W_{\text{expert}} / B_{\text{HBM}}$ | Only activated experts loaded |
| $\Delta i$ (retrieval) | $(S_{\text{ext}} \cdot h \cdot \text{bytes\_per\_param}) / B_{\text{HBM}}$ | Context transfer cost |

### C.4 Calibration Protocol (Proposed)

The coefficients $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are hardware-specific and should be calibrated via the roofline model:
$$ \alpha = \frac{1}{F_{\text{peak}}}, \qquad \beta = \frac{1}{B_{\text{HBM}}} $$
In practice, the effective $\alpha$ and $\beta$ will differ from peak values due to utilization inefficiencies. We propose to calibrate them by measuring wall-clock latency for each action type on the target hardware and fitting the linear model $C(a) = \alpha C_{\text{FLOP}}(a) + \beta C_{\text{HBM}}(a)$ via least squares.

